00:03:08.699 --> 00:03:13.574
Um, so today we will look at
why PDFs can become a barrier to

00:03:13.574 --> 00:03:17.732
accessibility, how assistive technology
interact with them, and what we can

00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:19.285
do to make them more accessible.

00:03:21.574 --> 00:03:26.907
So imagine, um, your website,
um, passed WCAG 2.1 AA.

00:03:27.377 --> 00:03:29.130
Your color contrast is perfect.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.147
Your keyboard navigation is flawless.

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:32.777
You've done everything right.

00:03:32.825 --> 00:03:37.325
Your developers worked on accessibility,
your designers chose accessible colors,

00:03:37.354 --> 00:03:41.022
and your content team added meaningful
alt text and works pretty well with

00:03:41.022 --> 00:03:42.941
screen reader user, screen readers.

00:03:43.574 --> 00:03:46.658
But then a user needs
your financial report, job

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.608
application, or privacy policy.

00:03:49.079 --> 00:03:52.001
They download the PDF, and
suddenly all that work on the

00:03:52.002 --> 00:03:54.082
website means absolutely nothing.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.167
The PDF might be untagged, have no proper
reading order, contain inaccessible

00:03:59.198 --> 00:04:03.679
tables, or even be a scanned image
that a screen reader can't read at all.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.417
And this isn't an occasional problem.

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:11.755
Um, around 85 percentage of PDFs
fail basic accessibility checks.

00:04:12.642 --> 00:04:16.584
The problem is that PDFs are often
treated as an afterthought, but for a

00:04:16.584 --> 00:04:21.403
screen reader user, that PDF is still
an essential content, and if it isn't

00:04:21.404 --> 00:04:23.847
accessible, it can become a major barrier.

00:04:24.344 --> 00:04:26.292
So that's the accessibility paradox.

00:04:26.492 --> 00:04:30.714
An accessible website can still
deliver an inaccessible experience.

00:04:33.791 --> 00:04:39.080
Um, so today we'll look at the PDF trap,
how assistive technology, um, read PDFs

00:04:39.107 --> 00:04:41.120
and the common barriers users face.

00:04:41.651 --> 00:04:45.251
We'll then cover the testing rem-
testing and remediation and when to

00:04:45.252 --> 00:04:50.425
choose PDF or HTML, and finish off
with some, um, practical takeaways

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.813
So now let's start by
understanding who actually gets

00:04:55.813 --> 00:04:58.039
excluded by inaccessible PDFs.

00:04:58.529 --> 00:05:03.272
For a screen reader user, imagine,
um, for the first, for-- The first one

00:05:03.272 --> 00:05:05.044
is, for example, a screen reader user.

00:05:05.544 --> 00:05:09.840
Imagine downloading a 50-page report
and hearing, "Graphic, graphic,"

00:05:09.864 --> 00:05:13.948
and again, "Graphic." No headings,
no structure, no way to navigate.

00:05:14.431 --> 00:05:19.623
For someone using magnification at
30% or, or 300 percentage or 400

00:05:19.629 --> 00:05:24.096
percentage zoom, a fixed-layout PDF
can actually mean constantly scrolling

00:05:24.097 --> 00:05:25.894
sideways just to read each line.

00:05:26.457 --> 00:05:28.892
The next one is motor impairment users.

00:05:29.131 --> 00:05:31.837
For someone with a motor
impairment, a poor tab order can

00:05:31.837 --> 00:05:33.825
actually make a form frustrating.

00:05:34.179 --> 00:05:38.135
Focus might jump from field 1 to
field 17 and then back to field 3.

00:05:38.757 --> 00:05:42.945
And next is the cognitive
and neurodiverse, uh, users.

00:05:43.253 --> 00:05:47.756
For people with cognitive and neurodiverse
us- uh, users, clear headings and

00:05:47.756 --> 00:05:49.400
structures provide a mental map.

00:05:49.799 --> 00:05:53.111
Without them, navigating a long
document can actually feel like

00:05:53.111 --> 00:05:55.246
being in a city with no street signs.

00:05:55.924 --> 00:06:00.410
Now, this matters because disability
is not a small or edge case audience.

00:06:00.786 --> 00:06:05.192
Around one in six people actually
experience significant disability.

00:06:05.621 --> 00:06:08.791
That's more than a billion people
who can be affected by whether

00:06:08.806 --> 00:06:10.433
our digital content is accessible.

00:06:10.782 --> 00:06:13.991
So when a PDF isn't accessible,
we are potentially excluding a

00:06:13.991 --> 00:06:15.900
significant part of our audience.

00:06:18.402 --> 00:06:20.601
So now let's look at the
scale of this problem.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.533
So 2.5 trillion PDFs are
actually created every year.

00:06:25.126 --> 00:06:30.095
That's an enormous number, and a PDF
is clearly not going anywhere away.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.256
But here's the problem, around
85 percentage of PDFs fail

00:06:34.292 --> 00:06:35.880
basic accessibility checks.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:39.341
That means billions of documents
can actually create barriers

00:06:39.349 --> 00:06:40.657
for people with disability.

00:06:41.129 --> 00:06:46.905
And 73 percentage of organizations do not
have a clear PD- PDF accessibility policy.

00:06:47.485 --> 00:06:51.281
We have accessibility policies
for websites and other apps,

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:53.487
but PDFs are often forgotten.

00:06:54.336 --> 00:06:56.325
Now, that's a strong business case too.

00:06:56.429 --> 00:07:00.341
The global disability market
is estimated at $13 trillion.

00:07:01.138 --> 00:07:03.090
So let's put these numbers together.

00:07:03.628 --> 00:07:09.539
2.5 trillion, uh, trillion PDFs, 85
percentage of failing accessibility

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:15.816
checks, 73 percentage of organizations
without a clear policy, and 13 trillion,

00:07:16.042 --> 00:07:18.433
uh, mar- thir- $13 trillion market.

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:23.763
Now, these numbers actually tell us that
PDF accessibility isn't a small problem.

00:07:24.062 --> 00:07:28.751
It's a significant problem, and a, and
even more a significant opportunity.

00:07:29.499 --> 00:07:32.369
So why are PDFs so difficult
to make it accessible?

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:38.231
Now, let's see, uh, let's actually see
how screen reader interact with a PDF.

00:07:42.488 --> 00:07:44.608
So it actually starts with
the document structure.

00:07:44.726 --> 00:07:50.346
Every property… every, uh, properly
built PDF can actually contain a hidden,

00:07:50.455 --> 00:07:55.475
um, tag tree, a semantic layer that
actually describes the heading, paragraph,

00:07:55.671 --> 00:08:01.072
um, list, tables, and forms, which is
invisible to sighted user, but it's

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.328
extremely essential to everyone else.

00:08:03.751 --> 00:08:07.852
Assistive technology tools
like NVDA, JAWS, or VoiceOver

00:08:07.852 --> 00:08:09.218
don't read the visual page.

00:08:09.636 --> 00:08:13.406
They actually query that tag tree
through the operating system acc-

00:08:13.515 --> 00:08:18.616
operating system's accessibility API to
actually retrieve the document semantic.

00:08:19.564 --> 00:08:24.941
Um, the screen reader then pulls out
the actual text along with the role

00:08:24.942 --> 00:08:30.027
information, which could be headings,
list items, or forms, and any other

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.679
alternative text which is, uh, sourced
directly from this particular tag tree.

00:08:35.361 --> 00:08:39.336
And finally, that content gets
synthesized as speech or, uh,

00:08:39.355 --> 00:08:41.115
even routed to a Braille display.

00:08:41.653 --> 00:08:45.646
Uh, even navigation commands like
pressing H to jump between headings

00:08:45.668 --> 00:08:52.784
or T to, um, jump between, um, tables
rely entirely on these tags or tag

00:08:52.809 --> 00:08:55.580
tree being present and being correct.

00:08:57.065 --> 00:09:02.321
Um, so no tag tree basically means, um,
no semantic information, and with no

00:09:02.321 --> 00:09:07.644
semantic information, the s- uh, the
screen reader can only, um, can only guess

00:09:07.664 --> 00:09:10.292
or even kind of fall silent completely.

00:09:14.329 --> 00:09:17.713
So now let's take a closer look
at the tag tree itself, uh, the

00:09:17.714 --> 00:09:19.558
hidden architecture of the PDF.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.145
Um, on the left is an example
structure of a tag tree.

00:09:24.679 --> 00:09:28.598
Um, everything should sit under the
document tree, uh, document root.

00:09:29.214 --> 00:09:34.150
Uh, inside that there are part tags which
divide the document into logical sections.

00:09:34.199 --> 00:09:39.472
Within section one, you can see an H1
heading reading Annual Report 2024,

00:09:39.476 --> 00:09:43.853
followed by a paragraph tag with body
text and a figure tag containing a

00:09:43.858 --> 00:09:48.601
chart with its alt text, uh, attached,
uh, directly to that, um, uh, to

00:09:48.602 --> 00:09:50.557
that image, uh, to that figure.

00:09:50.954 --> 00:09:55.404
Section two then continues with, with
an H2 heading, uh, Financial Overview,

00:09:55.510 --> 00:10:00.228
which is followed by an actual, um,
table tag with table rows containing

00:10:00.252 --> 00:10:06.092
TH tags for headers like Quarter and
Revenue, and TD tags for the data itself.

00:10:06.537 --> 00:10:11.082
There's also a list tag for key
highlights, and section three contains

00:10:11.108 --> 00:10:13.215
a form tag for interactive fields.

00:10:14.054 --> 00:10:19.622
Um, so now here are some of the, um,
key accessibility cons, uh, concept that

00:10:19.622 --> 00:10:22.135
this particular structure, um, enables.

00:10:22.587 --> 00:10:24.352
The first one is semantic mapping.

00:10:24.876 --> 00:10:30.367
Mm, tags actually define the, um, role
of each element, whether it is a heading,

00:10:30.402 --> 00:10:33.202
whether it is a list, a table, a form.

00:10:33.881 --> 00:10:35.804
Uh, the second one is reading order.

00:10:36.156 --> 00:10:40.447
The tag tree actually establish the
exact logical sequence, um, a screen

00:10:40.449 --> 00:10:45.074
reader mu-- screen readers would
follow, uh, which is independent of

00:10:45.101 --> 00:10:46.958
the visual placement of the text.

00:10:47.361 --> 00:10:51.983
Um, alternative text, uh, for image
is attached directly to images and

00:10:51.983 --> 00:10:53.610
charts within this tree itself.

00:10:54.047 --> 00:10:58.069
And structural navigation, this is what
let users jump between heading levels.

00:10:58.523 --> 00:11:02.713
Uh, one important distinction to
actually remember here is that tags

00:11:02.827 --> 00:11:04.504
provide the programmatic structure.

00:11:04.814 --> 00:11:06.095
They are not bookmarks.

00:11:06.198 --> 00:11:09.915
Um, bookmarks are actually
vis- actually visual navigation

00:11:09.944 --> 00:11:14.092
aid for sighted users clicking
through the, through a PDF viewer.

00:11:14.626 --> 00:11:19.583
Mm, tags are what assistive technology
actually depends on to understand, um,

00:11:19.632 --> 00:11:23.046
and communicate to the, communicate
the document to screen reader

00:11:23.046 --> 00:11:25.270
user or assistive technology user.

00:11:29.769 --> 00:11:32.812
So now let's look at, um, some
of the, some of the common

00:11:32.834 --> 00:11:34.824
accessibility barriers in PDF.

00:11:35.488 --> 00:11:39.762
Um, the first one, uh, being missing
or broken document structure.

00:11:40.214 --> 00:11:42.625
So now here's a problem, um, structurally.

00:11:43.126 --> 00:11:47.666
Um, most PDFs are missing the
document root tag entirely.

00:11:47.964 --> 00:11:53.603
Mm, content that should be read out
as, um, real meaningful text get tagged

00:11:53.603 --> 00:11:57.767
as artifact, which tells assistive
technology to completely ignore.

00:11:58.251 --> 00:12:03.233
Mm, documents are often missing part
or, um, section containers that would

00:12:03.233 --> 00:12:05.367
actually, um, organize the content.

00:12:05.809 --> 00:12:11.280
Everything ends up, um, being tagged
gener-generically as a paragraph,

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:15.586
regardless of whether it's actually
a heading or a list or a caption.

00:12:16.141 --> 00:12:20.223
Mm, and headers and footers aren't
properly marked as artifacts, so they

00:12:20.223 --> 00:12:24.265
don't get filtered out the way they
should be, uh, to a screen reader or

00:12:24.266 --> 00:12:26.722
an assistive technology, um, tools.

00:12:27.665 --> 00:12:30.592
Now, here's what that actually
means for a sc- uh, for a user.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:35.117
The screen reader actually end up
reading the header text out loud, um,

00:12:35.182 --> 00:12:37.637
on every single page over and over.

00:12:38.115 --> 00:12:43.168
Mm, there's no way an, a user can
navigate by section or topic because

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.957
the structure simply doesn't exist.

00:12:45.382 --> 00:12:50.092
Um, the whole document ends
up feeling like, uh, one giant

00:12:50.154 --> 00:12:52.169
undifferentiated block of text.

00:12:52.649 --> 00:12:56.689
Page numbers and watermarks, um,
interrupt the reading flow at random

00:12:56.689 --> 00:13:02.006
points, and ultimately, users can't
build a mental model of the document.

00:13:02.404 --> 00:13:06.543
They can't tell where, where they
are, what's coming next, or how

00:13:06.544 --> 00:13:08.668
the content is actually organized.

00:13:09.545 --> 00:13:14.457
Document structure is basically the
foundation, um, on which everything

00:13:14.463 --> 00:13:15.970
ac- everything else sits on.

00:13:16.476 --> 00:13:21.557
If the structure itself is broken, no
amount of alt text on images or table

00:13:21.595 --> 00:13:26.793
tagging actually, uh, would work or
make up for a broken document structure.

00:13:31.012 --> 00:13:32.646
So how do we actually fix this?

00:13:32.666 --> 00:13:36.765
Here are the, um, here are some
recommended actions for compliance.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:40.165
First is to ensure document is a root tag.

00:13:40.192 --> 00:13:43.159
Every piece of content should
sit within that structure.

00:13:43.597 --> 00:13:46.173
Second is to manage artifacts properly.

00:13:46.513 --> 00:13:49.924
That means, um, keeping the
headers, footers, page numbers,

00:13:49.924 --> 00:13:54.279
and waterma-ma-marks, um, should
be tagged as artifacts so screen

00:13:54.292 --> 00:13:57.939
readers can actually skip over
them while actual content is not.

00:13:58.582 --> 00:14:02.716
The third one is to establish,
um, semantic boundaries, um, using

00:14:02.748 --> 00:14:06.222
part tags and section containers
to actually organize the document

00:14:06.249 --> 00:14:10.849
logically so users can navigate, uh,
by structure through the document.

00:14:11.382 --> 00:14:15.225
The fourth one is, um, apply
appropriate semantic tags.

00:14:15.255 --> 00:14:19.732
Um, headings should be tagged as headings,
lists as lists, tables as tables.

00:14:20.157 --> 00:14:23.173
Not everything should be flattened
into a generic paragraph tag.

00:14:23.751 --> 00:14:28.604
And fifth, um, which is to perform, um,
fifth and the last one is to perform

00:14:28.604 --> 00:14:30.788
structural, um, quality control.

00:14:31.247 --> 00:14:34.644
Once the tagging is done, go back
and actually validate it, because a

00:14:34.683 --> 00:14:39.344
tag tree that looks right on paper
can still contain gaps that only

00:14:39.344 --> 00:14:40.882
show up when you actually test it.

00:14:41.242 --> 00:14:45.920
So get these five right and you
have actually built a foundation the

00:14:45.921 --> 00:14:50.167
rest of the document's accessibility
can actually, um, fall onto or,

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:51.983
you know, it can actually stand on.

00:14:56.921 --> 00:14:59.105
The second barrier is heading hierarchy.

00:14:59.429 --> 00:15:03.485
This one's subtle because visually the
document can actually look perfectly fine.

00:15:04.006 --> 00:15:08.035
The problem lives entirely in how
headings are tagged underneath.

00:15:08.603 --> 00:15:13.897
Um, on the left, you can see, um, it is
a broken hierarchy, heading hierarchy.

00:15:14.435 --> 00:15:19.198
Uh, the 18-point bold text is
correctly tagged as H1, but then the

00:15:19.198 --> 00:15:23.721
16-point bold text jumps straight
to H3, skipping H2 entirely.

00:15:24.284 --> 00:15:29.438
14-point bold text gets tagged as another
H1, which is a duplicate, um, when it-

00:15:29.499 --> 00:15:31.353
when ideally it should be a lower level.

00:15:31.937 --> 00:15:36.943
12-point bold, uh, text jumps all
the way to H5, skipping H3 and

00:15:36.953 --> 00:15:39.221
H4, um, heading levels in between.

00:15:39.794 --> 00:15:43.934
A regular body text, which is a
paragraph text, get mistakenly, uh,

00:15:43.977 --> 00:15:49.089
tagged as an H2, which creates a
false, um, false sense of heading.

00:15:49.762 --> 00:15:54.411
Um, and the last, uh, one
more, mm, 18-point bold heading

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.129
isn't tagged, um, at all.

00:15:56.565 --> 00:16:01.091
To a screen reader user navigating
by headings, this, uh, format

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:02.578
can be very disorienting.

00:16:02.906 --> 00:16:07.003
Um, the outline jumps around
unpredict-predictably or important

00:16:07.011 --> 00:16:08.889
sections do not show up at all.

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:13.820
Now, on the right, the same visual
document is tagged correctly.

00:16:14.107 --> 00:16:18.347
18-point bold is H1,
um, Annual Report 2024.

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:22.944
The 18-point… Um, the 16-point,
uh, bold text which is followed

00:16:22.961 --> 00:16:25.201
by the H1 is marked as H2.

00:16:25.709 --> 00:16:30.525
The 14-point bold is then,
um, text is again marked as H3

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.497
and so f- uh, and followed on.

00:16:33.122 --> 00:16:37.334
Um, the body text is correctly
tagged, tagged as a plain paragraph,

00:16:37.405 --> 00:16:41.808
and the last 16-point bold heading
is given as actual heading rather

00:16:41.842 --> 00:16:44.022
than giving it as a normal text.

00:16:44.346 --> 00:16:48.755
So this is the right, um,
hierarchy, heading hierarchy for

00:16:48.755 --> 00:16:51.505
this particular, um, tag tree.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:59.931
So now let's see why heading
hierarchy actually matters so

00:16:59.936 --> 00:17:01.966
much to assistive technology user.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:08.512
The navigation found in, uh, foundation
is this, um, screen readers, screen reader

00:17:08.513 --> 00:17:14.281
users don't, um, read documents top to
bottom, just like how a sighted, uh,

00:17:14.282 --> 00:17:16.816
users would often skim through a document.

00:17:17.334 --> 00:17:19.644
They actually navigate by heading level.

00:17:19.984 --> 00:17:23.175
That is jumping from heading to
heading to build a mental map of

00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:27.406
the document before actually diving
into a specific, um, section.

00:17:27.938 --> 00:17:33.633
When that hierarchy breaks, um, user
runs into, uh, very frustrating patterns.

00:17:34.058 --> 00:17:39.054
Mm, for example, broken navigation
sounds, uh, like, where did the H2 go?

00:17:39.405 --> 00:17:42.591
When a level is actually skipped,
users kind of lose their sense of

00:17:42.591 --> 00:17:43.799
where they are in the structure.

00:17:44.456 --> 00:17:50.329
Um, outline confusion sounds like, which
one is the real title in the document?

00:17:50.675 --> 00:17:54.838
When there are multiple, um, multiple
headings or duplicate headings or

00:17:54.847 --> 00:17:59.276
misused heading level, it's unclear,
uh, for a user what's actually

00:17:59.276 --> 00:18:01.182
the document's, um, main heading.

00:18:01.906 --> 00:18:06.725
Uh, and semantic noise sounds like,
why is this paragraph, um, announced

00:18:06.725 --> 00:18:09.756
as a heading when body text is…

00:18:09.813 --> 00:18:12.466
body text gets mistakenly
tagged as a heading?

00:18:12.982 --> 00:18:18.683
Mm, it interrupts navigation with, um,
false landmarks for a screen reader user.

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:24.255
Now, the primary mechanism to actually
remember here is that proper heading

00:18:24.256 --> 00:18:30.488
hierarchy is the number one navigation,
um, mechanism for any screen reader user.

00:18:31.145 --> 00:18:36.169
Get this right and you have given
screen reader user a reliable

00:18:36.195 --> 00:18:40.157
way to actually move through
the document on their own terms.

00:18:44.349 --> 00:18:46.985
Now, the third barrier
is, um, reading order.

00:18:47.409 --> 00:18:51.218
This one especially is very
disorienting because it happens

00:18:51.241 --> 00:18:53.417
completely invisible to sighted user.

00:18:54.033 --> 00:18:59.147
On the left here, um, you can see the
visual order, what you actually see on the

00:18:59.147 --> 00:19:04.590
page: a sidebar image, the main article
headline, the main article body text,

00:19:04.609 --> 00:19:06.586
um, and a footer with a page number.

00:19:06.992 --> 00:19:10.846
This follows a natural, um, left
to right, top to bottom flow.

00:19:11.246 --> 00:19:14.969
It matches how, um, human
eyes visually scan a page.

00:19:15.373 --> 00:19:18.834
But now on the right is the tag
order, what assistive technology

00:19:18.836 --> 00:19:22.600
actually reads, and it can tell
a completely different story.

00:19:22.997 --> 00:19:26.868
In this particular example, the screen
reader starts with the footer and its

00:19:26.885 --> 00:19:31.805
page number, then moves to the sidebar
image with no alt text, then the main

00:19:31.807 --> 00:19:36.611
article's body text, and only at the
end does it actually reach the headline.

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:41.559
That order is actually arbitrary
based on when the elements were

00:19:41.559 --> 00:19:46.610
created in the source document,
that is the, um, Word or the Word

00:19:46.618 --> 00:19:48.237
document that they would have used.

00:19:48.875 --> 00:19:51.979
Uh, it is not based on how
they actually appear visually.

00:19:52.619 --> 00:19:56.477
Um, often it's completely backward,
and in this case, a screen reader

00:19:56.490 --> 00:20:01.261
actually hears a footer first, even
before they even know what the article,

00:20:01.399 --> 00:20:03.061
uh, they're reading or what it is.

00:20:04.433 --> 00:20:08.673
This is exactly why visual inspection
isn't enough to confirm accessibility.

00:20:08.758 --> 00:20:13.059
A document, um, can look perfectly
ordered and can still be read,

00:20:13.263 --> 00:20:17.634
um, in a completely scrambled se-
sequence for a screen reader user.

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:23.416
So why does reading order
go wrong in the first place?

00:20:23.514 --> 00:20:27.347
It usually comes down to how
the document was actually built.

00:20:27.586 --> 00:20:31.123
Mm. The first one is,
um, multi-column layouts.

00:20:31.137 --> 00:20:32.696
Multi-column layouts are a big one.

00:20:33.024 --> 00:20:37.144
When they are created without, um, any
awareness of the underlying structure,

00:20:37.502 --> 00:20:42.107
the tag order of the PDF often just
follows, um, the raw creation sequence

00:20:42.109 --> 00:20:46.780
and not the actual, um, visual, uh,
columns and actual visual sequence.

00:20:47.784 --> 00:20:52.381
Um, sidebar content is frequently
added after the main content, uh, which

00:20:52.383 --> 00:20:57.601
means it can get inserted into the tag
order in the wrong place, even though

00:20:57.614 --> 00:21:00.093
visually it sits off onto the side.

00:21:00.671 --> 00:21:06.518
In tools like InDesign or Word, um, text
boxes are often placed, um, in whatever

00:21:06.518 --> 00:21:13.213
order felt natural during the layout and
not exactly in the order that, uh, should

00:21:13.227 --> 00:21:15.644
be actually be read by a screen reader.

00:21:16.269 --> 00:21:21.901
Um, form fields, uh, are usually
included, uh, at the end as a final

00:21:21.931 --> 00:21:26.076
step, and so their tab order frequently
doesn't match the visual layout the

00:21:26.076 --> 00:21:27.836
user ends up seeing on the screen.

00:21:28.547 --> 00:21:30.619
The last one is the copy-pasted content.

00:21:30.647 --> 00:21:33.920
Copy-pasted content carries,
um, its original creation

00:21:33.928 --> 00:21:35.716
timestamp into the tag order.

00:21:36.151 --> 00:21:40.388
So content pasted in from elsewhere
can actually jump the que- queue.

00:21:40.845 --> 00:21:46.066
Uh, it could appear out of, um, sequence
from where it actually, uh, visually sits.

00:21:46.606 --> 00:21:51.591
So if you notice the pattern, um,
of the issues of the common… of

00:21:51.591 --> 00:21:57.058
the reading order, um, co- issues,
none of these are actually, um,

00:21:57.183 --> 00:21:59.768
really, um, intentional mistakes.

00:22:00.022 --> 00:22:03.923
They're just side effects of how
documents get built, which is

00:22:03.923 --> 00:22:08.605
exactly why reading order needs to
be checked, um, and not assumed.

00:22:12.677 --> 00:22:17.232
Our fourth common barrier is images
without meaningful alt text, and

00:22:17.232 --> 00:22:19.684
this shows up in few different ways.

00:22:20.281 --> 00:22:22.274
First, the image-based PDF.

00:22:22.925 --> 00:22:26.545
This happens when a document
is scanned, um, rather than

00:22:26.545 --> 00:22:28.226
actually created digitally.

00:22:28.645 --> 00:22:32.833
The whole page then becomes
a one, um, giant image.

00:22:33.263 --> 00:22:36.004
To a screen reader, there is
no text at all, just a picture.

00:22:36.027 --> 00:22:37.668
The user gets absolutely nothing.

00:22:38.379 --> 00:22:41.670
Second one, what I call
is the decorative charts.

00:22:41.818 --> 00:22:42.967
This is sneakier.

00:22:43.642 --> 00:22:49.140
Um, someone build a complex, um,
infographic, um, maybe with multiple,

00:22:49.203 --> 00:22:54.501
um, data points, data categories, and
actually, um, different data relationship,

00:22:54.919 --> 00:22:58.780
but it is all tagged as a decorative
figure with absolutely no alt text.

00:22:59.457 --> 00:23:03.181
The visual is doing a lot of
communication or communicator work,

00:23:03.271 --> 00:23:07.478
uh, to a visual user, but a screen
reader user has no idea what it says.

00:23:08.286 --> 00:23:10.587
And third, the text as image.

00:23:10.822 --> 00:23:14.306
Headings, logos or call-outs
saved as image files instead

00:23:14.308 --> 00:23:15.921
of real selectable text.

00:23:16.563 --> 00:23:21.407
It might look fine visually, but it
can't be read aloud, resized or even

00:23:21.424 --> 00:23:22.926
picked up by a screen reader at all.

00:23:23.726 --> 00:23:27.954
So here are the common thread that you
will notice is that if it's an image

00:23:28.387 --> 00:23:32.109
and it is conveying information, it
should absolutely have an alt text

00:23:32.389 --> 00:23:37.023
that actually describes the information
and not just some, um, random file

00:23:37.023 --> 00:23:39.193
name or, or absolutely nothing at all.

00:23:43.035 --> 00:23:48.656
So now let's talk about, um, some of the
best practices for alt text in PDFs and,

00:23:48.793 --> 00:23:54.091
um, it completely depends on the type of
image that is being used in the document.

00:23:54.812 --> 00:23:57.091
Um, the first one is informative image.

00:23:57.102 --> 00:23:59.634
For informative images, be specific.

00:24:00.096 --> 00:24:07.062
Instead of, um, tagging it as or naming it
as chart or some random name as image001,

00:24:07.581 --> 00:24:09.716
um, describe what it actually shows.

00:24:09.729 --> 00:24:14.655
For example, if it is a charge… chart,
um, if it is a chart, make sure that you

00:24:14.656 --> 00:24:19.540
are including, um, something like bar
chart showing quarterly revenue growth

00:24:19.562 --> 00:24:21.935
from $8.2 million to $12.4 million.

00:24:22.911 --> 00:24:27.336
The alt text should actually,
um, convey the data and not just

00:24:27.345 --> 00:24:30.810
the la- and not just providing a
label to that particular object.

00:24:32.513 --> 00:24:37.638
Um, for decorative images, um, like
borders, backgrounds, or any other

00:24:37.638 --> 00:24:42.721
decorative photos that add absolutely
no value or information, don't

00:24:42.726 --> 00:24:46.280
force screen readers to actually
announce every visual element.

00:24:46.662 --> 00:24:50.225
These should be marked as a decorator
and should be skipped entirely

00:24:50.233 --> 00:24:52.350
for assistive technology users.

00:24:53.418 --> 00:25:00.134
Uh, for complex diagrams, um, alt text
alone often isn't, um, very useful.

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:04.918
Use a link to reference an extended
description elsewhere rather than

00:25:04.919 --> 00:25:11.068
trying to, um, cram a lot of information
into a 255 character alt text.

00:25:11.164 --> 00:25:15.815
Um, better to give a user
link and reference that.

00:25:16.582 --> 00:25:19.521
Um, and the last one is text-as image.

00:25:19.624 --> 00:25:24.457
Mm, for text-as image, um,
like a company logo, mm, never

00:25:24.879 --> 00:25:27.274
leave that text unannounced.

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:33.955
Um, provide something like, um, Acme
Co-Corporation, um, company logo or

00:25:33.956 --> 00:25:38.633
Acme Corporation logo, um, and it
actually ensures that it is… When

00:25:38.634 --> 00:25:44.170
you are giving a alt text to a company
logo, it actually ensures that it is,

00:25:44.306 --> 00:25:48.788
um, not invisible to a screen reader
user or an assistive technology user.

00:25:49.904 --> 00:25:54.757
Um, so the rule of thumb here
again is that match the alt text

00:25:54.898 --> 00:25:56.293
to the purpose of the image.

00:25:56.840 --> 00:26:00.514
Um, the purpose of the image could
be whether it is informative,

00:26:00.675 --> 00:26:05.299
whether it is a decorator, whether
it is complex, um, charts or graphs,

00:26:05.328 --> 00:26:08.168
or whether it is a textual image.

00:26:08.999 --> 00:26:12.050
So depending on that, you
need to match the alt text.

00:26:17.565 --> 00:26:20.820
Um, the next barrier
is inaccessible tables.

00:26:21.071 --> 00:26:23.536
Mm, on the left we have a broken table.

00:26:24.268 --> 00:26:28.050
Every cell including the ta- uh,
including the header is actually tagged

00:26:28.099 --> 00:26:35.292
as plain table, um, data cells or TD.
Visually, it looks absolutely fine.

00:26:35.451 --> 00:26:41.058
Um, product Q1, Q2 across the
top, widget A and B below.

00:26:41.485 --> 00:26:44.510
But to a screen reader,
there's no difference between

00:26:44.541 --> 00:26:46.027
a header and a data cell.

00:26:46.507 --> 00:26:52.822
So when a user navigate to, um, the text
120 or the number 120, they have no way

00:26:52.822 --> 00:26:55.848
of knowing that's widget A's Q1 number.

00:26:56.259 --> 00:26:57.892
The relationship is lost.

00:26:58.472 --> 00:27:01.781
On the right, the accessible
version actually fixes this.

00:27:02.273 --> 00:27:07.128
Header cells are tagged as TH with
scope attributes defining whether they

00:27:07.128 --> 00:27:09.697
are, um, column headers or row headers.

00:27:10.329 --> 00:27:14.053
Now, when a screen reader lands on
that same 120, it, it can actually

00:27:14.053 --> 00:27:20.011
announce widget A Q1 120 because the
semantic structure tells it exactly

00:27:20.017 --> 00:27:24.434
how that cell, um, can… how that
cell is actually related to its header.

00:27:25.463 --> 00:27:28.503
Um, so it's not enough for a
table to actually look right,

00:27:28.514 --> 00:27:29.752
it has to be built right.

00:27:29.809 --> 00:27:34.368
With real header tags, um, screen
readers can actually convey those, um,

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:39.447
same relationship which visual users
can actually scan and with their eyes.

00:27:43.289 --> 00:27:47.169
Um, so now beyond the basic
header tagging, there are a few

00:27:47.171 --> 00:27:51.344
more critical gaps, um, that
kind of show up in PDF tables.

00:27:52.109 --> 00:27:54.576
Um, some of them are merged cells.

00:27:54.607 --> 00:27:56.491
Um, merged cells are a common one.

00:27:56.493 --> 00:28:00.076
Without proper span attribute,
screen readers kind of lose

00:28:00.079 --> 00:28:02.907
count of how many columns or
rows they're actually navigating.

00:28:03.507 --> 00:28:06.117
Mm, nested tables cause a similar problem.

00:28:06.125 --> 00:28:09.535
Screen readers can't determine the
parent-child relationship between

00:28:09.541 --> 00:28:10.943
the outer and the inner table.

00:28:11.438 --> 00:28:13.099
The structure kind of falls apart.

00:28:13.483 --> 00:28:16.283
Sometimes tables aren't
used for data at all.

00:28:16.425 --> 00:28:18.224
They're used purely for visual layout.

00:28:18.629 --> 00:28:21.796
That creates semantic confusion
because a screen reader assumes

00:28:21.798 --> 00:28:25.664
a table means table of data, and
it tries to announce it that way.

00:28:26.047 --> 00:28:30.991
For complex, um, data tables, a missing
tables, um, summary is another gap.

00:28:31.384 --> 00:28:36.139
Users benefit from, um, a short
description of the table structure and,

00:28:36.477 --> 00:28:40.406
um, understand the purpose before they
actually dive into the data itself.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:43.598
And finally, providing empty header cells.

00:28:44.031 --> 00:28:48.693
Th tags that exist structurally but
contain absolutely no, um, text,

00:28:49.091 --> 00:28:52.814
um, that's almost worse than no
header at all since it actually

00:28:52.817 --> 00:28:56.609
signals, um, structure without
actually delivering any information.

00:28:58.055 --> 00:29:02.389
Now, each of these, um, break the
experience in a very different way for

00:29:02.390 --> 00:29:07.775
a screen read-- screen reader user, but
they all come down to the same root issue:

00:29:08.306 --> 00:29:13.557
structure that looks right without the
underlying, um, markup to support it.

00:29:17.216 --> 00:29:22.441
Um, the next barrier, mm, that we
would be discussing here is, uh, forms.

00:29:23.237 --> 00:29:28.329
Typical field failures that we actually
see in forms are, mm, a flat form,

00:29:28.692 --> 00:29:33.580
um, that is text box that look like
fields but are just rectangles, so

00:29:33.580 --> 00:29:35.731
users can't actually enter data.

00:29:36.283 --> 00:29:40.271
Even when fields are interactive, uh,
some of these fields do not have labels

00:29:40.281 --> 00:29:44.252
for a screen reader user to understand
what's the purpose of those fields.

00:29:44.624 --> 00:29:47.118
Uh, it would just announce as edit blank.

00:29:47.711 --> 00:29:53.885
Um, another… It basically does not
give, uh, any information to users.

00:29:54.514 --> 00:29:59.450
Uh, incorrect tab order can send,
um, focus jumping from name straight

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:03.653
to the submit f- uh, submit button
in the form, skipping, um, maybe

00:30:03.656 --> 00:30:05.166
four to five fields in between.

00:30:05.808 --> 00:30:11.202
Um, fields often lack descriptions like
whether it is a required field or a format

00:30:11.207 --> 00:30:14.666
hints and, uh, error messages go unstated.

00:30:15.094 --> 00:30:18.850
Now, if there are group fields like
radio buttons or a group of checkboxes,

00:30:18.864 --> 00:30:22.138
if they are not… if they aren't
wrapped in a field set, uh, with

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:25.562
a shared label, those relationship
is also lost for screen reader.

00:30:25.987 --> 00:30:29.998
And custom JavaScript validation can
actually trap keyboard users, preventing

00:30:29.999 --> 00:30:32.946
them from, preventing them from
actually moving through the form at all.

00:30:33.821 --> 00:30:37.087
Um, so now here's how we can
actually fix all of this problem.

00:30:37.401 --> 00:30:41.186
Start with Acrobat's prepare form
tool to actually auto-detect and

00:30:41.186 --> 00:30:42.963
actually create interactive fields.

00:30:43.334 --> 00:30:48.544
Set a descriptive tool tip for every field
that then, that b- that actually b- makes

00:30:48.544 --> 00:30:51.479
it, um, that becomes its accessible name.

00:30:52.993 --> 00:30:56.363
Uh, verify the tab order match the, um…

00:30:56.477 --> 00:31:00.115
verify that the tab order actually
match the, mm, visual flow.

00:31:00.746 --> 00:31:05.133
Add, um, clear descriptions
with, um, format requirements.

00:31:05.967 --> 00:31:11.732
Um, group related fields, um,
especially radio buttons or checkboxes,

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:14.037
um, under shared group names.

00:31:14.528 --> 00:31:19.517
And finally, test everything with both
keyboard-only navigation and a screen

00:31:19.519 --> 00:31:21.453
reader before you actually publish.

00:31:22.051 --> 00:31:25.739
The message here is a form
isn't accessible just because

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:27.480
it looks, um, fillable.

00:31:27.903 --> 00:31:32.518
It has to actually work for someone
who can't see or who can't click it.

00:31:35.321 --> 00:31:39.292
So now let's talk about our
testing toolkit because catching

00:31:39.316 --> 00:31:43.507
all these barriers, um, takes
more than, um, one tool.

00:31:44.413 --> 00:31:46.227
Um, so let's start with
automated checkers.

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:51.340
Some of these checkers like, mm, Adobe's
Acrobat Pro built-in accessibility

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:58.674
checker, um, the PAC 3, uh, PDF
validator, axe DevTools Pro, um, and

00:31:58.689 --> 00:32:04.228
CommonLook PDF can all flag structural
issues quickly and consistently.

00:32:05.081 --> 00:32:08.429
But automated testing is
just the starting point.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:11.427
You also need to test with
actual screen readers.

00:32:11.877 --> 00:32:16.602
That means, um, testing it with
NVDA and JAWS on Windows, uh,

00:32:16.623 --> 00:32:22.225
VoiceOver on Mac, Narrator on
Windows and, um, TalkBack on Android.

00:32:22.741 --> 00:32:26.957
Each one can be used slightly differently,
so testing across a few can actually

00:32:26.958 --> 00:32:28.683
give you a real- realistic picture.

00:32:29.226 --> 00:32:31.278
And finally, manual inspection.

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:35.677
This includes reviewing the tag panel
directly, checking reading order, testing

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:40.823
keyboard-only navigation, verifying the
color contrast, zooming the content to 200

00:32:40.823 --> 00:32:45.103
to 400 percentage, and actually walking
through f- uh, form field for tab order.

00:32:45.809 --> 00:32:49.101
So the takeaway here is that no single
tool can actually catch everything.

00:32:49.118 --> 00:32:54.830
Automated checkers find only about 30%
of accessibility issues, which means

00:32:54.831 --> 00:32:59.546
that screen reader testing is not just
optional, but it is also very mandatory

00:33:01.854 --> 00:33:03.983
So how does all of this
actually come together?

00:33:04.634 --> 00:33:09.498
Here I have put in a full PDF
remediation workflow in six steps.

00:33:10.045 --> 00:33:12.937
Um, step one is source audit.

00:33:13.942 --> 00:33:16.654
This happens even before
the PDF even exist.

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:23.914
Use proper word styles, structure content
correctly, uh, in InDesign and, um,

00:33:23.949 --> 00:33:26.654
avoid text as images from the very start.

00:33:27.324 --> 00:33:29.435
Step two is actually to auto-export.

00:33:29.678 --> 00:33:36.099
Mm. When converting to PDF, export
it with tags intact, uh, enable

00:33:36.105 --> 00:33:40.699
the PDF, uh, UA compliance, and
actually preserve the bookmarks.

00:33:41.301 --> 00:33:46.161
Um, step three is, um, making sure
that you are doing, uh, auto-check,

00:33:46.219 --> 00:33:50.678
run it through the Acrobat Checker
and the PAC 3 validator, and flag

00:33:50.776 --> 00:33:52.394
any critical errors that come up.

00:33:52.837 --> 00:33:54.547
Step four is manual fix.

00:33:54.585 --> 00:33:58.202
This is where you actually gets
on… get on hands-on, editing the

00:33:58.202 --> 00:34:01.349
tag panels directly, correcting the
reading order, and actually adding

00:34:01.385 --> 00:34:03.846
alt text, um, and table structure.

00:34:04.659 --> 00:34:08.540
Um, the step five is actually doing
screen reader testing, um, that is

00:34:08.554 --> 00:34:13.507
validating the document with, um,
NVDA or JAWS, um, do a keyboard-only

00:34:13.514 --> 00:34:18.263
testing, and even confirm, uh, form
validation actually works as expected.

00:34:18.902 --> 00:34:22.516
The last and final step is
publish and actually monitor.

00:34:23.130 --> 00:34:25.596
Accessibility doesn't end at launch.

00:34:25.735 --> 00:34:31.768
Mm. Maintain the version control, create a
user feedback loop, and actually schedule,

00:34:31.850 --> 00:34:37.989
um, regular re-audits to catch issues that
may emerge over time after you publish.

00:34:38.634 --> 00:34:41.387
So accessibility here
isn't a single checkbox.

00:34:41.733 --> 00:34:45.340
It's a workflow that starts at
creation and then continues along,

00:34:45.748 --> 00:34:48.587
um, along and long after publishing.

00:34:52.958 --> 00:34:56.724
So up till now, we've been talking
about fixes, um, after the document is

00:34:56.725 --> 00:34:59.381
already created or has already existed.

00:35:00.016 --> 00:35:03.324
But the biggest impact comes from
getting it right at the source,

00:35:03.422 --> 00:35:05.079
um, as I've mentioned previously.

00:35:05.567 --> 00:35:09.890
Mm, in Microsoft Word, that means
using the built-in, um, heading

00:35:09.890 --> 00:35:14.399
one through nine styles rather
than just, um, using bold text.

00:35:15.096 --> 00:35:19.472
Um, adding alt text to an image,
um, using table properties to

00:35:19.486 --> 00:35:23.852
add alt text and actually define
header, heading rows or header rows.

00:35:24.671 --> 00:35:29.421
Um, use- using real text formatting
instead of actually manually typing

00:35:29.421 --> 00:35:31.146
in hyphens or numbers for a list.

00:35:31.604 --> 00:35:35.702
Mm, setting the document title in
properties and before exporting, actually

00:35:35.712 --> 00:35:38.576
running the built-in accessibility
checker in a Microsoft Word.

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:43.621
Uh, in Adobe InDesign, use the
articles panel to actually define

00:35:43.622 --> 00:35:48.108
the reading order, map your paragraph
styles to PDF, to PDF tags through p-

00:35:48.141 --> 00:35:50.529
ver- through paragraph style option.

00:35:51.402 --> 00:35:55.332
Tag images as you export, and
set your export options to,

00:35:55.429 --> 00:35:57.881
uh, create proper tagged PDF.

00:35:58.573 --> 00:36:03.856
Uh, use the structure, uh, panel to
control tab orders and avoid, um, using

00:36:03.872 --> 00:36:11.418
any, um, flattening text to outline, uh,
that an actual, um, actually turn real

00:36:11.472 --> 00:36:13.876
text into, um, an unreadable shapes.

00:36:14.953 --> 00:36:16.928
So avoid using any flattening text.

00:36:17.003 --> 00:36:23.261
Um, so here's a rule of thumb to
actually remember: 80 percentage of PDF

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:27.586
accessibility is actually determined
by how the source document is created

00:36:27.931 --> 00:36:30.376
and not by how it is fixed afterward.

00:36:30.899 --> 00:36:34.664
So getting it right at the source
saves enormous w- rework down the line.

00:36:36.675 --> 00:36:40.837
So given everything we have covered
about PDF, um, accessibility challenge,

00:36:40.839 --> 00:36:44.583
it's naturally fair to ask, should we
even be using PDFs in the first place?

00:36:45.082 --> 00:36:46.529
Sometimes the answer is yes.

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:51.677
Fixed layout is essential in cases like
legal contrast-- contracts that need exact

00:36:51.679 --> 00:36:56.565
signature placement, um, architectural
drawing, um, drawings with precise

00:36:56.567 --> 00:37:01.409
measurements, or print-ready brochures
where design integrity actually matters.

00:37:01.875 --> 00:37:05.856
Um, there, there are also
regula-regulatory and legal requirements

00:37:05.909 --> 00:37:10.635
like court fillings, um, that need
exact page reproduction, government

00:37:10.638 --> 00:37:15.184
submission that require, um, you know,
PDF archival standards or documents that

00:37:15.184 --> 00:37:17.287
actually need, um, digital signature.

00:37:17.937 --> 00:37:21.870
Offline distribution is another valid
reason when users need access without

00:37:21.871 --> 00:37:26.096
internet connectivity, field workers
using tablets in remote locations.

00:37:26.588 --> 00:37:29.924
Uh, the last one is when there
are complex visual lay-layouts,

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:33.998
for example, magazines and annual
reports, design-heavy publication.

00:37:34.512 --> 00:37:38.908
Um, so here's a question worth
asking every time: Do I need a fixed

00:37:38.909 --> 00:37:40.951
layout or do I just need a document?

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:46.087
Most of the time, the hon- the honest
answer is the latter, and that's when

00:37:46.087 --> 00:37:51.016
a properly structured Word or an HTML
document will actually serve users better

00:37:51.367 --> 00:37:54.404
than a PDF could ever, uh, serve the user.

00:37:56.507 --> 00:38:00.006
So if PDF isn't always the
right call, when does ac- HTML

00:38:00.009 --> 00:38:01.476
actually serve users better?

00:38:01.737 --> 00:38:04.284
Let's walk through it
and through it quickly.

00:38:04.852 --> 00:38:10.764
Um, for forms, HTML gives you native
input labels, um, validation elements.

00:38:11.224 --> 00:38:14.398
PDFs tend to have flat forms
and broken ta- tab order.

00:38:14.454 --> 00:38:18.779
For da- uh, for data tables, HTML
can be responsive, sortable, and

00:38:18.779 --> 00:38:20.086
screen reader friend- friendly.

00:38:20.538 --> 00:38:24.734
While PDF tables are fixed layout and
require complex remediations to get right.

00:38:25.238 --> 00:38:28.549
For long documents, HTML
reflows to fit any screen.

00:38:28.694 --> 00:38:32.889
Uh, PDFs often force horizontal
scrolling at higher zoom levels.

00:38:33.386 --> 00:38:37.867
Um, for dynamic content, HTML supports
live updates, searching, and filtering.

00:38:38.358 --> 00:38:42.373
PDF is pretty static and requires
a fully, um, and would require full

00:38:42.379 --> 00:38:44.603
re-export every time something changes.

00:38:45.073 --> 00:38:50.155
Uh, for mobile users, HTML, uh, HTML's
responsive design adapts to the device.

00:38:50.583 --> 00:38:54.647
PDFs often turn into a pinch/zoom
nightmares on most phones.

00:38:55.099 --> 00:38:59.284
For search engines, HTML content
gets indexed and is SEO friendly,

00:38:59.607 --> 00:39:03.396
while PDFs are frequently, um,
invisible to search crawls.

00:39:03.779 --> 00:39:08.723
And for accessibility, accessibility
itself, HTML5 gives you, um, native

00:39:08.742 --> 00:39:10.966
semantic elements built in from the start.

00:39:11.373 --> 00:39:14.661
So PDFs require manual tag
creation every single time.

00:39:15.194 --> 00:39:16.772
So the pattern here is very clear.

00:39:16.888 --> 00:39:21.301
HTML is accessible by default, PDF
has to be made, um, accessible,

00:39:21.703 --> 00:39:23.747
and that's a much heavier lift.

00:39:25.516 --> 00:39:28.993
So before we wrap up, here's a
resource slide worth bookmarking,

00:39:29.035 --> 00:39:33.873
a starting toolkit for going deeper
into PDF testing and remediation,

00:39:33.933 --> 00:39:35.557
um, for your- on your own.

00:39:36.207 --> 00:39:42.724
Um, for testing tools, you've got, um,
Adobe Acrobat Pro DC, PAC, axe DevTools,

00:39:42.781 --> 00:39:45.326
um, NVDA, Color Contrast Analyzer.

00:39:46.176 --> 00:39:50.097
Um, for learning resources,
you can go to WebAIM.

00:39:50.272 --> 00:39:53.394
Um, you can, um, go to Deque University.

00:39:53.394 --> 00:39:55.527
You can actually go to Section 508.

00:39:55.878 --> 00:39:57.712
User PDF Techniques for WCAG.

00:39:57.884 --> 00:40:02.649
Mm. And for communities to actually
keep learning alongside others, there

00:40:02.650 --> 00:40:05.141
is the, um, Accessibility Slack.

00:40:05.204 --> 00:40:07.611
There is the PDF
Accessibility on LinkedIn.

00:40:08.082 --> 00:40:10.600
Um, there is a CSUN conference.

00:40:11.155 --> 00:40:16.049
Um, you don't really need to master all of
this today, but keep this slide, um, handy

00:40:16.384 --> 00:40:19.310
as a reference for the next time you're
actually working through an accessib-

00:40:19.798 --> 00:40:22.190
accessibility fix, which is pretty tricky.

00:40:24.154 --> 00:40:28.557
So now let's bring the session all
together with five key takeaways.

00:40:28.848 --> 00:40:33.018
Um, one, PDF are the silent
killers of inclusive design.

00:40:33.571 --> 00:40:37.995
They look finished, but for many users,
they are simply not reuse- not usable.

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:41.473
The second one, um, the
tag tree is everything.

00:40:41.596 --> 00:40:44.924
It's the invisible skeleton that
actually determines whether a document

00:40:44.926 --> 00:40:48.825
makes sense to a screen reader or
whether it would fall apart entirely.

00:40:49.332 --> 00:40:52.200
The third one is test with
real assistive technology.

00:40:52.451 --> 00:40:55.328
Automated checkers only catch
about a third of issues.

00:40:55.719 --> 00:40:58.761
Real screen readers and keyboard
testing is non-negotiable.

00:40:59.395 --> 00:41:01.455
Um, the fourth one is start at the source.

00:41:01.502 --> 00:41:05.408
The vast majority of accessibility
comes from how a document is built

00:41:05.412 --> 00:41:09.306
in Word or in InDesign, not from
the fixes applied a-afterward.

00:41:09.884 --> 00:41:13.185
And the last and final one is
it- is to actually default to

00:41:13.185 --> 00:41:15.698
HTML and justify the use of PDF.

00:41:16.198 --> 00:41:18.357
Do not use PDF simply out of habit.

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:23.274
Choose it when there's a real need, such
as a fixed layout or a legal requirement.

00:41:23.671 --> 00:41:27.506
Otherwise, HTML will usually provide
a more accessible experience.

00:41:27.797 --> 00:41:29.433
So that's the whole talk in fine line.

00:41:29.433 --> 00:41:32.622
Remember these, and you're already
ahead of most of what's out there.

00:41:34.225 --> 00:41:36.261
So yeah, um, thank you so much.

00:41:36.361 --> 00:41:38.499
That's pretty much all I wanted to cover.

